WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.075
Levinese is a field guide to the words Michael Levin uses when he talks

2
00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.480
about living intelligence.

3
00:00:05.921 --> 00:00:09.857
It is not his site, and Morph, the guide who answers your questions here,

4
00:00:10.008 --> 00:00:10.867
is not him.

5
00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:15.824
Everything you read is drawn from his published papers and talks, and every entry

6
00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.450
names the ones it came from.

7
00:00:17.891 --> 00:00:19.800
Here is how to use it, in two minutes.

8
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.193
Start with the dictionary.

9
00:00:21.344 --> 00:00:24.154
A hundred and nineteen terms, each one sourced.

10
00:00:24.328 --> 00:00:27.555
Type in the box at the top to narrow the list, or jump straight

11
00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.692
to a letter along the rail beside it.

12
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.453
The full entries are precise.

13
00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:33.299
They are also dense.

14
00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.132
So there is a switch at the top of the page.

15
00:00:36.829 --> 00:00:42.518
Choose Plain language, and every term becomes a few short sentences a curious

16
00:00:42.564 --> 00:00:43.899
twelve-year-old could follow.

17
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.440
Nothing is added that the full entry does not already say.

18
00:00:48.044 --> 00:00:52.270
Show the full entry brings the precision, and the sources, straight back.

19
00:00:56.287 --> 00:00:59.515
If a definition still leaves you with a question, ask.

20
00:01:00.118 --> 00:01:03.984
Under every term there are three ready-made questions and a box for one of

21
00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:04.831
your own.

22
00:01:05.157 --> 00:01:10.126
Morph reads Levin's work before answering, which takes fifteen to thirty seconds,

23
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.448
and it tells you while it is working.

24
00:01:12.454 --> 00:01:15.252
One more thing, and it is the part that matters most.

25
00:01:15.577 --> 00:01:18.770
If you think an entry is wrong, type that into Morph.

26
00:01:19.095 --> 00:01:21.208
Those messages are read by a person.

27
00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:25.596
Two definitions were corrected this way on the seventeenth of September, and the

28
00:01:25.655 --> 00:01:29.079
reader who caught them is named in the margin beneath each one.

29
00:01:33.096 --> 00:01:37.427
Behind the dictionary sits the corpus itself: the papers, the talks and the

30
00:01:37.461 --> 00:01:40.283
transcripts every definition was drawn from.

31
00:01:40.886 --> 00:01:44.195
Search it when you want the original, or simply wander.

32
00:01:44.196 --> 00:01:45.287
Read it simply.

33
00:01:45.798 --> 00:01:47.284
Go deeper when you want to.

34
00:01:47.795 --> 00:01:49.153
Ask when you are stuck.

35
00:01:49.281 --> 00:01:50.930
And tell us when we are wrong.
